---
// Figure: a reader stores an old value after the writer's delete (slow reader, or lagging replica).
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="inv-race" title="An old price comes back after the delete" controls="steps"
  caption="A sequence of steps, not a timed simulation. Every Redis command shown was run on Redis 8.10 in this order, with the same replies; the database steps are the application's. In the replica version, the lag of a few hundred milliseconds is illustrative.">
  <Choice slot="params" name="cause" label="The gap comes from" value="slow"
    options={[{ value: 'slow', label: 'a slow reader' }, { value: 'replica', label: 'a lagging replica' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>A slow reader: the reader misses product:42 and reads £39 from the database, then stalls. The writer commits £35 and deletes product:42, which removes nothing because Redis has no copy yet. The reader resumes and stores £39 with a one-hour TTL. Every read for the next hour is a hit that returns £39, unless the price changes again first.</p>
    <p>A lagging replica: the writer commits £35 on the primary and deletes product:42. A reader then misses and reads the row from a replica that has not yet received the change, so it gets £39 and stores it with a one-hour TTL. The replica catches up moments later, but Redis already holds £39 and serves it for an hour. No pause is needed: the replica's lag opens the gap.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { switchSequence } from './switch-sequence.mjs';
  import { raceSpecs } from './sequences.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="inv-race"]').forEach(root => switchSequence(root, raceSpecs, 'cause'));
</script>
